# The Coalton commit this tree is built and tested against.
#
# Coalton is consumed as a git checkout tracking `main`, not a Quicklisp release
# (see docs/coalton-upstream.md). That means each machine tracks upstream
# INDEPENDENTLY -- and two machines on different Coalton commits is a
# reproducibility hazard that shows up as "works on mine, not on yours" with no
# visible cause.
#
# This file is the fix: the REPO declares which Coalton it is on, so adopting a
# new one is an ordinary commit that propagates through git like everything else.
#
# To sync a machine to this pin:
#   cd "$(sbcl --noinform --non-interactive \
#            --eval '(require :asdf)' \
#            --eval '(load (merge-pathnames "quicklisp/setup.lisp" (user-homedir-pathname)))' \
#            --eval '(ql:quickload :coalton :silent t)' \
#            --eval '(princ (namestring (asdf:system-source-directory :coalton)))')"
#   git fetch origin && git checkout <sha below>
#
# Then rebuild: sbcl --dynamic-space-size 4096 --script bootstrap.lisp
# If anything looks impossible afterwards, clear ~/.cache/common-lisp and retry.
#
# A `git pull` OF THIS TREE is itself a trigger, which is the part that surprises
# people. A changed Coalton source forces its dependents to recompile, and a rebuild
# interrupted partway leaves the cache in a state where the next load FAILS rather
# than resuming where it stopped. So a Coalton compile error arriving straight after
# a pull is very likely a half-built cache and not the change you just pulled: clear
# the cache before you go looking at the diff. On 2026-09-21 one Coalton file changed
# on main -- mnemosyne/src/field.lisp -- and a consuming app hit this; the first read
# was that the merges had broken something, which is the wrong place to start. Three
# cold builds that day were all clean, because a COLD cache and a HALF-BUILT one are
# different things and only the first was ever being tested.
#
# Resolving the checkout through ASDF rather than a hard-coded path is what makes
# this work identically on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
#
# OPTIONAL `repo` LINE. A sha is only meaningful relative to a repository, so if we
# ever work from a FORK, name it here and scripts/setup.{sh,ps1} will clone from it:
#
#   repo    https://github.com/<org>/coalton
#
# Absent, upstream (coalton-lang/coalton) is assumed. setup warns if an existing
# checkout tracks a different remote than this file declares -- otherwise a machine
# cloned from upstream and one cloned from the fork can silently disagree about what
# a given sha refers to.
#
# To verify that the pinned Coalton is the one that actually LOADS (which the
# checkout alone does not prove -- ASDF may resolve elsewhere, or serve stale
# fasls):  sbcl --dynamic-space-size 4096 --script scripts/check-coalton.lisp

sha     7915fad0
date    2026-07-29
subject Add Optional & Result instances for Foldable

# --- staying current -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Coalton is a Lisp library with no native code and no network surface, so its
# risk profile is nothing like a TLS or transport library's. It is listed here
# anyway because the check is about the PRACTICE, not the danger: a pin that
# opts out is a pin nobody reviews, and the exemption outlives the reasoning.

advisories https://github.com/coalton-lang/coalton/security/advisories
reviewed   2026-09-15
